    solution: 'SELECT a.provider_id, COUNT(d.diagnosis_id) AS diagnosis_count\nFROM appointments a\nJOIN diagnoses d ON a.appt_id = d.appt_id\nGROUP BY a.provider_id\nORDER BY diagnosis_count DESC',
    debrief: 'Dr. Smith (provider 1) has 7 diagnoses, followed by Dr. Patel and Dr. Lee with 4 each. The JOIN from appointments to diagnoses is needed because diagnoses only stores appt_id — provider_id must be retrieved through the appointment. Note that six no-show appointments produce no diagnoses, so appointment count (10 for provider 1) differs from diagnosis count (7).\n\n**Forensic trap:** Querying directly from the diagnoses table with GROUP BY provider_id — but diagnoses only has appt_id, not provider_id. A user who writes SELECT provider_id, COUNT(*) FROM diagnoses GROUP BY provider_id would get an error (no such column) or, if they confuse the join direction and count from appointments without filtering to matched diagnoses, returns appointment counts (10 for provider 1) instead of diagnosis counts (7). This over-count is silently wrong for quality measurement purposes.\n\n**Sanity check:** Confirm 6 rows (one per provider). Verify provider 1 diagnosis_count = 7 (10 appointments minus 3 no-shows that are in the diagnoses-matched set — actually provider 1 has 4 no-shows but 7 diagnoses means 7 completed visits had coded diagnoses, not all 6 completed). Cross-check: providers 4, 5, 6 should have 2, 2, 1 diagnoses respectively.',
