    isFree: false,
    tags: ['DENSE_RANK', 'PARTITION BY', 'CTE', 'window function', 'content analytics'],
    roles: ['PA', 'DA', 'PM'],
    priority: 1,
    estimatedMin: 12,
    datamartId: 'consumer',
    prompt: 'The content strategy team wants to know how each piece of content ranks within its own category by total interactions — not globally, but relative to other content in the same category. Return content_id, category, interaction_count, and category_rank. Handle ties fairly. Order by category, then category_rank.',
    expectedColumns: ['content_id', 'category', 'interaction_count', 'category_rank'],
    expectedRowCount: 8,
    hints: ['Build a CTE that joins interactions to content and counts per content_id. Then use DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY interaction_count DESC) in the outer query — PARTITION BY resets the rank for each category.'],
    checkValues: [{ content_id: '1', category: 'fitness', interaction_count: '8', category_rank: '1' }],
    solution: 'WITH content_counts AS (\n  SELECT c.content_id, c.category, COUNT(*) AS interaction_count\n  FROM interactions i\n  JOIN content c ON i.content_id = c.content_id\n  GROUP BY c.content_id, c.category\n)\nSELECT content_id, category, interaction_count,\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY interaction_count DESC) AS category_rank\nFROM content_counts\nORDER BY category, category_rank, content_id',
    debrief: 'Content 1 (fitness video) dominates its category at 8 interactions — 2.7× more than content 6 (3 interactions). Categories with only one piece (finance, tech) auto-rank at 1, which is less useful for benchmarking. The PARTITION BY category clause resets the rank counter for each category — without it, you get a single global ranking instead of per-category. DENSE_RANK() handles ties without gaps: if two items had the same count, both get rank 1 and the next gets rank 2. RANK() would assign 1,1,3 — skipping rank 2. ROW_NUMBER() would assign 1,2 arbitrarily, breaking ties. The CTE is required because you cannot reference the interaction_count alias inside the DENSE_RANK window function in the same SELECT without first materializing it. Weak answer: a subquery per category to find its local max — does not generalize to N categories and breaks on ties.\n\n**Forensic trap:** Using RANK() instead of DENSE_RANK() in this dataset — since there are no ties in interaction_count per category, the output is numerically identical. Both return 1 for the top item in each category. This means the wrong function choice is invisible in testing and only surfaces when real data produces a tie. A user who ships RANK() will pass code review here but will get wrong results in production when two items share the same interaction count.\n\n**Sanity check:** Confirm 8 rows (all 8 content items appear). Verify content 1 (fitness) shows interaction_count = 8, category_rank = 1. Verify content 6 (fitness) shows interaction_count = 3, category_rank = 2. Check content 2 (finance) and content 5 (tech) each appear with category_rank = 1 (single item in their categories).',
    sqliteNote: null,
