    solution: 'SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) AS buyer_count\nFROM orders',
    debrief: 'The bug is COUNT(*) vs COUNT(DISTINCT user_id). COUNT(*) counts rows — there are 28 order rows, so it returns 28. COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) counts each buyer once regardless of how many orders they placed, returning 12. The buyer activation rate is 12/15 = 80%, not 187%.\n\n**Why it passed review:** The metric name was "buyer_count." The query returns a count. Without knowing the expected range (buyer_count can never exceed the user count), the output looks like a valid engagement metric.\n\n**The sanity check:** buyer_count can never exceed SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users = 15. Any buyer_count > 15 is impossible and signals COUNT(*) was used. This is the first thing to check when a metric produces a number that exceeds its natural ceiling.',
