    checkValues: [{ user_id: '5', order_count: '5' }],
    solution: 'SELECT user_id, COUNT(*) AS order_count\nFROM orders\nGROUP BY user_id\nHAVING COUNT(*) >= 3\nORDER BY order_count DESC',
    debrief: 'The bug is the missing HAVING clause. Without it, GROUP BY returns one row per user for all users who have any order — 12 users. With HAVING COUNT(*) >= 3, only users 1 (3 orders), 3 (3 orders), and 5 (5 orders) qualify — the 3 true power buyers.\n\n**Why the wrong output looks plausible:** The query produces a valid engagement ranking. The loyalty team assumes the report already filters — they\'re reading a ranked list and looking at relative order counts, not verifying the filter is applied. The report header says "Power Buyers" but the query has no threshold.\n\n**WHERE vs HAVING:** WHERE COUNT(*) >= 3 is a syntax error — WHERE runs before GROUP BY and COUNT doesn\'t exist yet. HAVING runs after aggregation and is the only correct place for this filter. This is one of the most common SQL mistakes at the analyst level.',
