    solution: 'SELECT s.sub_id, s.account_id, s.ended_at, s.mrr\nFROM subscriptions s\nWHERE s.status = \'churned\'\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM subscriptions s2\n    WHERE s2.account_id = s.account_id\n      AND s2.status = \'active\'\n  )\nORDER BY s.ended_at',
    debrief: 'The bug is not in the SQL syntax — it\'s in the analytical frame. "Churned subscription" ≠ "lost customer." Account 1 has a churned Business sub AND an active Enterprise sub (the upgrade path). Account 3 has a churned Business sub AND an active Growth sub (a downgrade, not true churn). The win-back list must exclude any account with a current active subscription.\n\n**This is the most important forensic class:** the query is syntactically correct and the output is a valid dataset. The error is in what the analyst believes the query measures versus what it actually measures.\n\n**The fix:** NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM subscriptions s2 WHERE s2.account_id = s.account_id AND s2.status = \'active\'). The three truly churned accounts are 13, 14, and 15 — no active subscription anywhere. Sub 14 (account 13, Business at $999) is the first in the result.\n\n**Production rule:** Before any customer-facing campaign list ships, verify every account on it does not have an active relationship. A win-back email to an active customer is a credibility incident.',
