    expectedRowCount: 1,
    hints: [
      'Monthly growth compounding means each month\'s growth applies to the new base, not the original. What SQL function handles exponentiation?',
      'At 15% monthly growth: month 1 = 13.8, month 2 = 15.9, month 3 = 18.3... the base keeps growing.',
      'Use POWER(1.15, 6) instead of (1 + 0.15 * 6). POWER(1.15, 6) ≈ 2.313 vs additive 1.90.',
    ],
    checkValues: [{ current_users: '12', projected_6mo_users: '28' }],
    solution: 'SELECT\n  COUNT(*) AS current_users,\n  ROUND(COUNT(*) * POWER(1.15, 6)) AS projected_6mo_users\nFROM users',
    debrief: 'The bug is using linear (additive) growth instead of compound growth. The correct model applies each month\'s rate to the new base — not to the original.\n\n**The math with 12 current users at 15% monthly growth:**\n- Linear: 12 × (1 + 0.15 × 6) = 12 × 1.90 = **22.8 → 23**\n- Compound: 12 × 1.15^6 = 12 × 2.313 = **27.76 → 28**\n\nAt a 5-user undercount this looks modest. But scale the error: at 1,000,000 users with 15% monthly growth, the linear model predicts 1.9M vs the compound 2.3M — a 400,000-user undercount. Infrastructure provisioned for 1.9M will fail under 2.3M load.\n\n**Why linear growth is tempting:** it is arithmetically simple and produces reasonable-looking numbers at short horizons. The error compounds with time: at month 1, linear gives 1.15× and compound gives 1.15× — identical. At month 6, they diverge by 22%. At month 12, linear gives 2.8× and compound gives 5.35× — nearly a 2× gap.\n\n**The rule:** any projection involving a periodic growth or decay rate must use compound multiplication — POWER(1 + rate, periods). Linear models (rate × periods) are only valid when growth is truly additive (e.g., flat headcount additions per month), never when growth is percentage-based.',
    sqliteNote: null,
