    solution: 'SELECT ROUND(\n  COUNT(CASE WHEN ended_at >= \'2024-01-01\' AND ended_at < \'2024-04-01\' THEN 1 END) * 1.0 /\n  NULLIF(COUNT(CASE WHEN started_at < \'2024-01-01\'\n    AND (ended_at IS NULL OR ended_at >= \'2024-01-01\') THEN 1 END), 0)\n, 3) AS q1_churn_rate\nFROM subscriptions',
    debrief: 'The bug is the denominator. Churn rate = churned / at-risk-at-start. The broken query uses end-of-period active count (12) as the denominator — but the 3 subscribers who churned in Q1 are no longer in that count. They were excluded from the denominator even though they are the subjects of the measurement.\n\n**The math:** Q1 2024 had 14 subscribers active at the start (started before Jan 1, not yet ended). Three churned. Correct churn = 3/14 = 21.4%. Broken = 3/12 = 25.0%.\n\n**Why the broken version overstates churn:** the more subscribers churn, the smaller the end-of-period denominator, and therefore the higher the apparent churn rate. If 6 subscribers had churned, broken = 6/8 = 75% while correct = 6/14 = 43%. The metric is self-inflating — bad months look worse than they are.\n\n**The standard definition:** monthly or quarterly churn rate = subscribers churned in period / subscribers active at start of period. Never use the ending count as the denominator.\n\n**The production impact:** churn rate is used to calculate LTV, payback period, and CAC targets. An overstated churn rate produces an understated LTV, which can incorrectly justify cutting acquisition spend or pricing changes that hurt growth.',
