    debrief: 'The bug is using delivered_at instead of picked_up_at as the end timestamp. The metric is labeled "restaurant prep time" but it actually measures acceptance-to-delivery — prep time + courier travel time combined.\n\n**The result:** avg prep time appears to be 51 minutes, when actual kitchen prep averages 21 minutes. The 30-minute gap is courier travel. Restaurants are being penalized in performance dashboards for delivery times they have no control over.\n\n**The order lifecycle timestamps:**\n1. created_at — customer places order\n2. accepted_at — restaurant accepts, kitchen starts cooking\n3. picked_up_at — food is ready, courier departs restaurant\n4. delivered_at — customer receives order\n\nPrep time = accepted_at → picked_up_at. Delivery time = picked_up_at → delivered_at. End-to-end time = accepted_at → delivered_at.\n\n**Why this happens:** all four timestamps exist and look similar in the schema. When the analyst wrote the first version of the query, they chose the "end state" timestamp (delivered) instead of the "prep complete" timestamp (picked_up). The query ran without errors and returned plausible-looking numbers.\n\n**The detection rule:** always cross-validate a time-based metric against a known ground truth from a different source. Restaurant ticket printers record prep time — if that source says 20 minutes and the query says 50, the query is wrong.',
