    priority: 1,
    estimatedMin: 10,
    datamartId: 'swiggy',
    beforeWriting: 'Should cancelled orders count toward GMV? State your answer before writing the WHERE clause — this determines whether your total matches what the business actually received.',
    prompt: 'Growth wants to understand how much of Swiggy\'s delivered GMV comes from Swiggy One subscribers vs non-subscribers. For each customer segment (grouped by is_swiggy_one), show the number of delivered orders and total revenue. Order by is_swiggy_one descending (subscribers first).',
    expectedColumns: ['is_swiggy_one', 'order_count', 'total_gmv'],
    expectedRowCount: 2,
    hints: ['What does one row represent? One customer segment (Swiggy One vs non). JOIN orders to customers on customer_id. Filter to delivered orders only.', 'SUM(total_amount) gives you GMV. COUNT(*) gives order count. GROUP BY c.is_swiggy_one.'],
    checkValues: [{ is_swiggy_one: '1', order_count: '16', total_gmv: '5900' }, { is_swiggy_one: '0', order_count: '5', total_gmv: '1255' }],
    solution: 'SELECT c.is_swiggy_one,\n       COUNT(*) AS order_count,\n       SUM(o.total_amount) AS total_gmv\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = \'delivered\'\nGROUP BY c.is_swiggy_one\nORDER BY c.is_swiggy_one DESC',
    debrief: 'Swiggy One subscribers (6 of 10 customers) drove 16 of 21 delivered orders and ₹5,900 of ₹7,155 total GMV — roughly 82% of revenue from 60% of customers. This is a healthy premium-tier concentration.\n\n**Swiggy context:** Swiggy One is a subscription offering free deliveries and restaurant discounts. The key question for growth: is the 82% GMV concentration because subscribers order more frequently, or because they spend more per order? Both are true here — subscribers average 2.7 orders vs 1.25 for non-subscribers, and their average order value is also higher. If you were presenting this analysis, you\'d want to compute both metrics.\n\n**Interview trap:** Some candidates filter on both status = \'delivered\' AND a date range without being asked. Know what you\'re filtering and why before adding conditions.',
    sqliteNote: null,
