    solution: 'SELECT payment_mode, SUM(total_amount) AS total_revenue\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = \'delivered\'\nGROUP BY payment_mode\nORDER BY total_revenue DESC',
    debrief: 'Two independent bugs compound each other: wrong aggregation function (COUNT vs SUM) and missing status filter.\n\n**Bug 1 — COUNT vs SUM:** COUNT(*) counts rows. SUM(total_amount) sums money. Both run without error on any table. The alias "total_revenue" implies a sum, but the code does a count — a naming-aggregation mismatch the engine cannot catch.\n\n**Bug 2 — No status filter:** 4 of 25 orders are cancelled (orders 7, 10, 15, 20). Cancelled orders have no delivered revenue — including them overstates "how much we earned" if you were summing, and inflates counts here.\n\n**Correct output:** UPI: ₹4,000 (11 delivered orders), card: ₹2,760 (7 orders), COD: ₹395 (3 orders). Total ₹7,155.\n\n**Interview context:** This class of bug — COUNT when SUM was intended — is among the most common in real analyst code. Always read the alias, the aggregation function, and the WHERE clause together. If they disagree, the query is wrong.',
